MTU = Maximum Transmission Unit
MTU is the maximum size of an IP packet that can be transmitted over a network interface/link without fragmentation.
For example, if MTU = 1500 bytes, an IP packet normally cannot be larger than 1500 bytes on that link.
If the packet is larger than the MTU:
IPv4 + DF=0: Large packet → Fragmentation → Multiple smaller fragments
IPv4 + DF=1: Large packet → Drop → ICMP Fragmentation Needed
IPv6: Router cannot fragment the packet → Drop → ICMPv6 Packet Too Big
TCP: TCP usually uses MSS and Path MTU Discovery to choose an appropriate segment size, so fragmentation is normally avoided.
UDP: UDP does not use MSS. An application can create a large UDP datagram. If the path has a smaller MTU, the datagram may be fragmented in IPv4 or dropped if DF is enabled.
Important points:
MTU is measured in bytes.
MTU is a property of a network interface/link.
A switch normally does not make packets smaller.
An IPv4 router can fragment packets.
Fragments are reassembled at the destination.
ping -l 4000 in Windows means 4000 bytes of ICMP data, not the total IP packet size.
Path MTU is the smallest MTU along the entire path.
